-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}\\cdot 2-{2}^{2}\\cdot \\pi )-({0}^{2}\\cdot 0-{0}^{2}\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =(2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-104623/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104623"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124030/selection/4a4bb5db9dde0223a34576e3bb51c8af99a425786b3a1cc5bbc9a5a30b7ab232" :answer/id #uuid "8e00351c-154e-5f00-830e-9f21d11d242b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{16}+16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124030/selection/d242eb964d9d8b898c184b534cb8bc692e5635d5edaeeb348bf948dee06a62c8" :answer/id #uuid "c288d06b-1c5f-5646-82c7-7085020a717e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16-{e}^{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124030/selection/ddbbac54729b51db3df5f3a5a248585add29166e367b9fe1b81397fdbaf53dcf" :answer/id #uuid "768fb61a-94e5-5769-ae50-bdededd9cbd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{4}+12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124030/selection/c98cb181f74d878e513228215eb911b1879df94861388cc7ad269277138add1d" :answer/id #uuid "adbed09b-a536-5455-8d30-2f1555e14b92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{16}-{e}^{2}+12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124030/selection/8a113d55c652de4baa040feabfabeb1879ddcbefec2c72b7100db14150c22239" :answer/id #uuid "6273403b-8b52-5890-8003-da6df730eaec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{16}+{e}^{4}-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124030/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7fedfe95-c69e-527f-aa62-88847979916e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124030/selection/c98cb181f74d878e513228215eb911b1879df94861388cc7ad269277138add1d" :answer-field/choices ["q-124030/selection/4a4bb5db9dde0223a34576e3bb51c8af99a425786b3a1cc5bbc9a5a30b7ab232" "q-124030/selection/d242eb964d9d8b898c184b534cb8bc692e5635d5edaeeb348bf948dee06a62c8" "q-124030/selection/ddbbac54729b51db3df5f3a5a248585add29166e367b9fe1b81397fdbaf53dcf" "q-124030/selection/c98cb181f74d878e513228215eb911b1879df94861388cc7ad269277138add1d" "q-124030/selection/8a113d55c652de4baa040feabfabeb1879ddcbefec2c72b7100db14150c22239"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124030" :question/id #uuid "c26944f8-c26a-581e-af36-60ef1c01800e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124030" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{e}^{xy}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\frac{x}{2}=\\sqrt{y}$ for $y\\in [1,4],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\sqrt{t},t⟩,\\,t\\in [1,4].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2,1⟩, \\\\ r(4) & =⟨4,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(4))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f(4,4)-f(2,1) \\\\  & =({4}^{2}+{e}^{4\\cdot 4})-({2}^{2}+{e}^{2\\cdot 1}) \\\\  & =(16+{e}^{16})-(4+{e}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{16}-{e}^{2}+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-124030/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124030"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124035/selection/4b6cc012e900b92e404b8518d134cff6ad19d3b8036b1e99b32fbf7f082ae944" :answer/id #uuid "45bd400e-54db-5d8e-8a60-ba1a9010e45f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124035/selection/25896a72f5d013c50bb7cb627bf4b333dd1dedb6f0f4e897db2f1ea33bb9ec65" :answer/id #uuid "5e02cf51-7b56-59cf-a2d6-aa185409e0a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi -2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124035/selection/b37ea75c06020afb54de805105ac84cb2358ed10c8fa4f616cf2f776fe75d34a" :answer/id #uuid "efbdcde0-4fec-5fd1-bb55-9299434bf962" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124035/selection/35c394cc573b60d2bb7c2193dcafe355ef552facd637d86b868c8ee378d5b334" :answer/id #uuid "8fad278b-6872-5682-b7d8-31ee77033d78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-\\pi +1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124035/selection/30d771277e5f88c2a08a4e446b7ea080dd38abbdbbc820f6d698c4b2c0bc77a1" :answer/id #uuid "19aa44bd-1db2-58d8-98ba-1d87267c04af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi +1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124035/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b98d9688-2730-5c00-97b0-8c5ed274e5a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124035/selection/4b6cc012e900b92e404b8518d134cff6ad19d3b8036b1e99b32fbf7f082ae944" :answer-field/choices ["q-124035/selection/4b6cc012e900b92e404b8518d134cff6ad19d3b8036b1e99b32fbf7f082ae944" "q-124035/selection/25896a72f5d013c50bb7cb627bf4b333dd1dedb6f0f4e897db2f1ea33bb9ec65" "q-124035/selection/b37ea75c06020afb54de805105ac84cb2358ed10c8fa4f616cf2f776fe75d34a" "q-124035/selection/35c394cc573b60d2bb7c2193dcafe355ef552facd637d86b868c8ee378d5b334" "q-124035/selection/30d771277e5f88c2a08a4e446b7ea080dd38abbdbbc820f6d698c4b2c0bc77a1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124035" :question/id #uuid "620f1829-4215-5cda-9dd0-aa2a1afa5537" :question/math-academy-id "q-124035" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ for $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨-1,\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(-1,\\pi )-f(1,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}-(-1))-({0}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}+1)+1 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-124035/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124035"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-104617/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "a96bcabd-de03-5e05-bef7-ae66aece6e73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104617/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "0e70f472-a624-5040-ab16-34d1e6379894" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104617/selection/9b49ed1a91e9087b6b2cc4f15092d29a6f833d1b3fe90395a973a4df3f6d0b57" :answer/id #uuid "6b05b441-9d42-562c-87d9-8781c08a7b3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104617/selection/986e06f062dcc0ad37fbb185f7e0d56b0fd8e74b571035e7dc0af3d06af89951" :answer/id #uuid "f589185d-0128-571a-99b9-6127a6f05031" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104617/selection/6acadda2cf01d7393af5cf150300ca180189308ad197cceffca549e1b6e5368c" :answer/id #uuid "504cb021-2dc3-5db0-ba79-128d9b4f9867" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104617/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9d28a1bb-c094-553f-b16c-0efb855f6537" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-104617/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-104617/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-104617/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-104617/selection/9b49ed1a91e9087b6b2cc4f15092d29a6f833d1b3fe90395a973a4df3f6d0b57" "q-104617/selection/986e06f062dcc0ad37fbb185f7e0d56b0fd8e74b571035e7dc0af3d06af89951" "q-104617/selection/6acadda2cf01d7393af5cf150300ca180189308ad197cceffca549e1b6e5368c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-104617" :question/id #uuid "d9b52684-6943-5924-8a3c-2cbafe021e4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104617" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2x$ for  $x\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2\\pi )-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =(0+1)-(0+1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-104617/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104617"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-123879"] :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve ${y}^{2}=4x$ for $y\\in [0,2\\sqrt{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\sqrt{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(2\\sqrt{2}) & =⟨2,2\\sqrt{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2\\sqrt{2}))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(2,2\\sqrt{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =((2\\sqrt{2})^{2}-{2}^{2})-({0}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =(8-4)-(0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-124029/selection/6f89b9633c0803f4317d31d4df3091ed81dcb134f6b7f3cf8a9e3f990a61fbd0" :answer/id #uuid "39099231-0c3d-5a11-8a68-cbe52c64c2ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124029/selection/4b6cc012e900b92e404b8518d134cff6ad19d3b8036b1e99b32fbf7f082ae944" :answer/id #uuid "079a94dd-65cb-5f65-8f3f-0baedc6989d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124029/selection/2d423200ea52f8d1811b263b7d2b7e7fe459571df24e3ce9005e19ed496e12c3" :answer/id #uuid "76f80bc6-b64c-5e69-9cd5-c31404ea7848" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124029/selection/b627bdd0369562afa8f12be8f167039ff5f7a2bdc550b832dce7f5205e0a89bc" :answer/id #uuid "c4548094-3fff-562b-aa96-fa3be84bede2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124029/selection/8537c62f3693a15d710e124fc3a2ab0cd236506ccd7697c787ad7ea4f09b0f61" :answer/id #uuid "39222d4a-b564-5066-b817-07b4c9517222" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124029/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "44a93798-762e-56ff-bfd8-119414ed4f09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124029/selection/b627bdd0369562afa8f12be8f167039ff5f7a2bdc550b832dce7f5205e0a89bc" :answer-field/choices ["q-124029/selection/6f89b9633c0803f4317d31d4df3091ed81dcb134f6b7f3cf8a9e3f990a61fbd0" "q-124029/selection/4b6cc012e900b92e404b8518d134cff6ad19d3b8036b1e99b32fbf7f082ae944" "q-124029/selection/2d423200ea52f8d1811b263b7d2b7e7fe459571df24e3ce9005e19ed496e12c3" "q-124029/selection/b627bdd0369562afa8f12be8f167039ff5f7a2bdc550b832dce7f5205e0a89bc" "q-124029/selection/8537c62f3693a15d710e124fc3a2ab0cd236506ccd7697c787ad7ea4f09b0f61"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124029" :question/id #uuid "9108600d-d502-5925-a662-bd43df3a26bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-124029" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve ${y}^{2}=x$ for $y\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-124029/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124029"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-104761/selection/f1d490d2dd5f05d137d44e99bfd7e587fb7ec59231ac5b411704f688aea7174a" :answer/id #uuid "faf55122-699b-512c-beff-cf073616e47f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104761/selection/157ff791928ca4a623c08e006a60feed6cf984efe0493761a4651aa90e63cae4" :answer/id #uuid "690646f5-c7b7-5e45-94fd-1788aaebbf7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1+{ln}^{2}⁡3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104761/selection/9d663b1a1d5c4a63ea3f4f7498a2f573705729d80776a8db8f944322dc1fb789" :answer/id #uuid "729767aa-1ee8-565d-97b2-1cb4e8f4335e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104761/selection/88d95014e75af51acf35fab834524c38a764b46e738d9b811e24ab91c39fc61f" :answer/id #uuid "01f97d20-d223-5989-92e3-107a070088af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104761/selection/a36b84e5a4543e816b3295a29d8ccf409cc756d01ccbeab9fba2b247cf607ef5" :answer/id #uuid "b528c2f9-76d0-5505-a68c-a5eb60923cbf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104761/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "eaf821bc-7700-5009-b487-8ae8247fc5aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-104761/selection/f1d490d2dd5f05d137d44e99bfd7e587fb7ec59231ac5b411704f688aea7174a" :answer-field/choices ["q-104761/selection/f1d490d2dd5f05d137d44e99bfd7e587fb7ec59231ac5b411704f688aea7174a" "q-104761/selection/157ff791928ca4a623c08e006a60feed6cf984efe0493761a4651aa90e63cae4" "q-104761/selection/9d663b1a1d5c4a63ea3f4f7498a2f573705729d80776a8db8f944322dc1fb789" "q-104761/selection/88d95014e75af51acf35fab834524c38a764b46e738d9b811e24ab91c39fc61f" "q-104761/selection/a36b84e5a4543e816b3295a29d8ccf409cc756d01ccbeab9fba2b247cf607ef5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-104761" :question/id #uuid "e5ae920f-cea9-5c2f-a972-f3194327bb51" :question/math-academy-id "q-104761" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=2x{e}^{-y}\\,i+(2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y})\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ for $x\\in [2,3].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{2x{e}^{-y}}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{(2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y})}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2x{e}^{-y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2x{e}^{-y}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{-y}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)⟩,\\,t\\in [2,3].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨2,0⟩, \\\\ r(3) & =⟨3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(2,0)$ and $(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-f(2,0) \\\\  & =({3}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2})-({2}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-0}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2-4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-104761/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104761"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-104733/selection/d1989c5759ced112728422497475d12c2804922e42f725917a53e8619d0ea915" :answer/id #uuid "ea61c170-f603-5b21-b049-2fed1dce609e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9{ln}^{3}⁡2-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+21" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104733/selection/f852b590b172cac8f93eff6783f74a18ed0b473b9a88c9a1aa79076f67081006" :answer/id #uuid "334c4b6b-ed2b-5297-9f15-c5fbe2215313" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{ln}^{3}⁡2-63" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104733/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "d865b04d-9d85-56c7-acd4-46dc1ecb40a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104733/selection/ea41a68dcaf74c14547fc87173b811fffb33e67ea000d96f3553b6ac20fc8c87" :answer/id #uuid "3a68fbb3-a3d5-5d01-8a31-b0714ca2befc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡2+9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104733/selection/48af784d9f47d270e1e28e4f5c70cff0847d31ed17e676f2ddb20e72f68fc120" :answer/id #uuid "d40e8f9a-cc43-50c9-8b44-7449fc359e3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{ln}^{3}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104733/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b940db30-d4ac-58ed-9bdd-6c9560dc490b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-104733/selection/d1989c5759ced112728422497475d12c2804922e42f725917a53e8619d0ea915" :answer-field/choices ["q-104733/selection/d1989c5759ced112728422497475d12c2804922e42f725917a53e8619d0ea915" "q-104733/selection/f852b590b172cac8f93eff6783f74a18ed0b473b9a88c9a1aa79076f67081006" "q-104733/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-104733/selection/ea41a68dcaf74c14547fc87173b811fffb33e67ea000d96f3553b6ac20fc8c87" "q-104733/selection/48af784d9f47d270e1e28e4f5c70cff0847d31ed17e676f2ddb20e72f68fc120"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-104733" :question/id #uuid "8896c3f5-9f61-59e4-9099-67d4914ed9e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-104733" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,{t}^{2}⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=({x}^{2}-y)\\,i+({y}^{2}-x)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot 4)-(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3}-0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =9{ln}^{3}⁡2+\\frac{64}{3}-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =9{ln}^{3}⁡2-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-104733/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104733"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-104747/selection/b00cefaad7022534e78cd2fbfec7ddc9daa79494174d415c1e570b65b81e2b40" :answer/id #uuid "ae86b783-8132-527b-80a7-7bfbeffe2201" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡e-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104747/selection/f6b4c7b9502219e51c357c37d3d673b19fa46ddd46914da0a1e069780090790f" :answer/id #uuid "8c2b22a5-88cc-5dab-a28e-1c5f16cee8f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡e-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104747/selection/43ad98e7ee8f6ce63abe5e6f065ac283cd779020ca04eac19b781fe14e9d7a7e" :answer/id #uuid "4991d7f0-98fd-5667-9afb-016a4e2962b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(e-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104747/selection/2c536c82b0b7c5a0b2a747e3458cc366506361050fb28e54b3d2dfe70d041f8e" :answer/id #uuid "2ddb5671-0de7-52bc-8316-3297e6f1a1f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2e-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{e}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104747/selection/2794459a9319a59eb594434a9c698826fb918bdb753589d620cae8a9a8b6cc4a" :answer/id #uuid "76c445c2-a50e-5b23-b4d3-76c1801091e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104747/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a003a062-911c-5434-b258-0b0cceabcf8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-104747/selection/2794459a9319a59eb594434a9c698826fb918bdb753589d620cae8a9a8b6cc4a" :answer-field/choices ["q-104747/selection/b00cefaad7022534e78cd2fbfec7ddc9daa79494174d415c1e570b65b81e2b40" "q-104747/selection/f6b4c7b9502219e51c357c37d3d673b19fa46ddd46914da0a1e069780090790f" "q-104747/selection/43ad98e7ee8f6ce63abe5e6f065ac283cd779020ca04eac19b781fe14e9d7a7e" "q-104747/selection/2c536c82b0b7c5a0b2a747e3458cc366506361050fb28e54b3d2dfe70d041f8e" "q-104747/selection/2794459a9319a59eb594434a9c698826fb918bdb753589d620cae8a9a8b6cc4a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-104747" :question/id #uuid "6e3d391f-7795-50e6-b816-0c7d52038f87" :question/math-academy-id "q-104747" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,2⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},e⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},e).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},e)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡e)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡e-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-104747/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104747"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-104745/selection/d9194a8943f283c16fdc5b0236bafd10539b71231ffc9137655723b0af1ff060" :answer/id #uuid "b1a2648c-6304-52a3-bc17-35cd577a3403" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104745/selection/d0ab94490dee12664d2f70471811316d5faf74c9c2c3f6dde546976ea292b76b" :answer/id #uuid "bfffa47d-ab9d-5c4e-af0c-142558b126dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104745/selection/bf9da5eda33b0747c85c14cc2637fcb08975b2c6444398dcd67c40e1863751f8" :answer/id #uuid "0827866f-4490-56da-8495-2a7a9dd3ba93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104745/selection/a34ed0725a0669d0eb225b1a97b3a15288aa2f8616e8c295616aba0af09a3e38" :answer/id #uuid "21ca5670-1ec6-56f0-b1be-827cf09aa7f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104745/selection/8c0db5450c323ed841f8ca4e0fabac8c4bc96f736d5f0cd273f5d8fed42499f1" :answer/id #uuid "05b5193e-4d3a-5a68-9935-ab53b74b41a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104745/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4624752d-a286-530b-8f21-e6e14a5665f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-104745/selection/d9194a8943f283c16fdc5b0236bafd10539b71231ffc9137655723b0af1ff060" :answer-field/choices ["q-104745/selection/d9194a8943f283c16fdc5b0236bafd10539b71231ffc9137655723b0af1ff060" "q-104745/selection/d0ab94490dee12664d2f70471811316d5faf74c9c2c3f6dde546976ea292b76b" "q-104745/selection/bf9da5eda33b0747c85c14cc2637fcb08975b2c6444398dcd67c40e1863751f8" "q-104745/selection/a34ed0725a0669d0eb225b1a97b3a15288aa2f8616e8c295616aba0af09a3e38" "q-104745/selection/8c0db5450c323ed841f8ca4e0fabac8c4bc96f736d5f0cd273f5d8fed42499f1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-104745" :question/id #uuid "81e5d171-627c-5e94-a5b8-312641107175" :question/math-academy-id "q-104745" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=({e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,i+(x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y=x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{({e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{(x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,\\pi -1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(\\pi ,\\pi -1).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,\\pi -1)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(\\pi \\cdot {e}^{\\pi -1}+(\\pi -1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )-(0\\cdot {e}^{1}+1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =(\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}+0)-0 \\\\  & =\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-104745/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104745"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-104739/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "2b56c420-3592-5ff4-9ed2-d66ecebd37d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104739/selection/f6cd7ad815cbceebfe1cddfb3219edf7d8bce0273da1c741fc6acf433fc7d1c8" :answer/id #uuid "60a4bb50-1405-5ba3-9b0f-ed0daf2ac52d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104739/selection/ebe3381a5098af0b657f02ddf7a7e9a9547719c27d33a2417db7a11def846b94" :answer/id #uuid "b7edbd2c-c0d9-50d1-ae32-800849d75c41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi -1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104739/selection/e838212c72dad0dea3f17b9a551beacc79717b8a6770370fc6f2b7bc3d5ed460" :answer/id #uuid "c963e1f9-d5e8-5c5c-a72d-7160476c3bf1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{2}+\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104739/selection/7100452d413eaab2e8f1940209aac2f596215bf60156b63e0f9748e195de8c3a" :answer/id #uuid "526f7552-a18a-5e0d-924f-6a2a9e5cdc98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104739/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c70b6be5-6032-58b8-a7bc-73d5c1bc3f0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-104739/selection/7100452d413eaab2e8f1940209aac2f596215bf60156b63e0f9748e195de8c3a" :answer-field/choices ["q-104739/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-104739/selection/f6cd7ad815cbceebfe1cddfb3219edf7d8bce0273da1c741fc6acf433fc7d1c8" "q-104739/selection/ebe3381a5098af0b657f02ddf7a7e9a9547719c27d33a2417db7a11def846b94" "q-104739/selection/e838212c72dad0dea3f17b9a551beacc79717b8a6770370fc6f2b7bc3d5ed460" "q-104739/selection/7100452d413eaab2e8f1940209aac2f596215bf60156b63e0f9748e195de8c3a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-104739" :question/id #uuid "72b1f81d-cb45-57b0-98cf-3032506f331a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104739" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y})\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y={x}^{2}+{sin}^{2}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y})}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}+{sin}^{2}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}})-({0}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =(0+{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}})-(0+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-104739/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104739"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8759"] :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy^2+ 2xz.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\mathbf r(t)= \\langle e^{t}, t, e^{-t}\\rangle$ for $t\\in \\left[0,1\\right],$ evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\nabla f\\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0,1⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨e,1,\\frac{1}{e}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0,1)$ and $\\left(e, 1, \\dfrac{1}{e}\\right).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(e,1,\\frac{1}{e})-f(1,0,1) \\\\  & =(e(1)^{2}+2\\cdot e\\cdot \\frac{1}{e})-(1(0)^{2}+2(1)(1)) \\\\  & =(e+2)-2 \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9502"] :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)= xe^{y}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $x + 2y=3$ for $x\\in [-1,1],$ evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\nabla f\\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\dfrac{1}{2}(3-t)\\right\\rangle, \\quad t\\in[-1,1].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨-1,2⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(-1,2)$ and $(1,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(-1)) \\\\  & =f(1,1)-f(-1,2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot {e}^{1}-(-1)\\cdot {e}^{2} \\\\  & =e+{e}^{2} \\\\  & =e(1+e).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8760"] :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t,  1-t \\right\\rangle, \\quad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\displaystyle{\\int _C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r}$ for $\\mathbf F = ye^{xy}\\,\\mathbf i + xe^{xy}\\,\\mathbf j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $\\mathbf F = \\nabla f.$ Since we're given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =y{e}^{xy}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =x{e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)= e^{xy}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,0)-f(0,1) \\\\  & ={e}^{1\\cdot 0}-{e}^{0\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
